1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,370
If you live in England and have a mild infection, don't ask your GP for antibiotics.
如果你住在英格兰，并且有轻微感染，不要让你的家庭医生给你开抗生素。

2
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:10,230
He will probably refuse and give you something else instead.
他很可能会拒绝，然后给你开别的药。

3
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:17,390
This is part of a drive to put a stop to harmful infections becoming resistant to these drugs.
这是一项措施的一部分，旨在制止有害感染对这些药物产生耐药性。

4
00:00:17,390 --> 00:00:34,730
Recent figures from the NHS have shown that the number of prescriptions for all types of antibiotics were down by 7.3% on the previous year to about 34 million in 2015 and 2016, which is considered a success by the authorities.
英国国家医疗服务体系最近的数据显示，2015年至2016年，所有类型抗生素的处方数量比上一年下降了7.3%，约为3400万张，这被当局视为一项成功的举措。

5
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:59,430
Peter Taylor, Professor of Microbiology, UCL School of Pharmacy, says that antibiotics are probably "the safest and most effective drugs ever produced, but they have an Achilles heel - every time we use an antibiotic it alters the levels of bacteria out there in the environment and engenders resistance in a way that is very similar to Darwinian natural selection.
伦敦大学学院药学院的微生物学教授彼得·泰勒表示，抗生素可能是“有史以来最安全、最有效的药物，但它们有一个致命弱点——每次我们使用抗生素时，它都会改变环境中的细菌数量，并以一种与达尔文自然选择非常相似的方式产生耐药性”。

6
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:06,210
The power of these substances was discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928 almost by chance.
这些物质的功效是亚历山大·弗莱明在1928年几乎偶然间发现的。

7
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:15,880
The Scottish bacteriologist returned to his laboratory after a holiday to find a bacteria-free circle around a growth of mould on a Petri dish.
这位苏格兰细菌学家度假归来后，在实验室的培养皿中发现，霉菌周围有一圈没有细菌的区域。

8
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:19,890
It gave us our first antibiotic: penicillin.
这让我们有了第一种抗生素：青霉素。

9
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:24,380
The availability of these drugs revolutionised the treatment of infections.
这些药物的出现彻底改变了感染的治疗方式。

10
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:30,180
In the past STIs spread by bacteria were often incurable and deadly.
过去，由细菌传播的性传播感染往往无法治愈，而且会致命。

11
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:35,180
Today all it takes to treat them is a trip to the clinic and a course of antibiotics.
如今，治疗这些感染只需去一趟诊所，服用一个疗程的抗生素即可。

12
00:01:35,180 --> 00:01:40,940
Childbirth was very dangerous and routinely ended in death for both mother and baby.
过去，分娩非常危险，常常导致母婴双亡。

13
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:49,410
Now the risk of a woman dying in England and Wales during labour is between 40 and 50 times lower than 60 years ago.
现在，在英格兰和威尔士，女性在分娩时死亡的风险比60年前降低了40到50倍。

14
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:55,140
Bacteria will continue to evolve to seek survival and we have to up our game.
细菌将继续进化以谋求生存，我们也必须提升自己的应对能力。

15
00:01:55,140 --> 00:02:10,700
There hasn't been a new class of antibiotics since the 1980s and pharmaceutical companies are looking into a wide-range of alternatives - from compounds from insects to nanotechnology - to develop new weapons in the continuous war against superbugs.
自20世纪80年代以来，就再也没有出现过新一类的抗生素了。制药公司正在研究各种各样的替代方案——从昆虫体内的化合物到纳米技术——以便在与超级细菌的持续斗争中开发出新的“武器”。
